*TALKING POINTS: HILLARY CLINTON TOPS NEW NATIONAL POLL*



*BACKGROUND*:* A new **ABC News/Washington Post poll*
<http://abcnews.go.com/blogs/politics/2015/01/historys-a-positive-for-clint=
on-not-so-for-bush-or-romney/>*
conducted
January 12-15, 2015 finds that Hillary Clinton leads each of the top
potential GOP candidates in hypothetical matchups.*



*THE POLL:*

-          According to the poll, Hillary beats all other potential GOP
contenders polled by double digits.

 -          In head-to-head matchups, Clinton leads Jeb Bush 54%-41%, Mitt
Romney 55%-40%, Chris Christie 53%-40%, Rand Paul 54%-41%, and Mike
Huckabee 56%-39%.



*THE BIG PICTURE:*

=C2=B7         Hillary Clinton continues to connect with Democrats, Indepen=
dents
and Americans of all backgrounds who are hungry for her experience and
vision. Because of Hillary Clinton=E2=80=99s years of public service, her s=
trong
record as a leader, and her commitment to being a voice to the voiceless,
she has the support of so many Americans.

 =C2=B7         This latest poll is in line with what others polls and pund=
its
have been showing for months =E2=80=93 that Hillary Clinton has unprecedent=
ed
support behind her, despite the onslaught of Republican attacks against her=
.


o   Clinton is extremely popular among Democrats: an NBC/Marist poll
<http://www.msnbc.com/msnbc/hillary-clinton-beats-joe-biden-hypothetical-20=
16-matchup>
 from July 7-13, 2014  puts her favorability/unfavorability rating in Iowa
at 89 percent/6 percent; in New Hampshire it=E2=80=99s even higher, at
94 percent/4 percent.



o   In polls conducted this summer in Ohio
<http://www.quinnipiac.edu/news-and-events/quinnipiac-university-poll/ohio/=
release-detail?ReleaseID=3D2066>
, New Jersey
<http://www.quinnipiac.edu/news-and-events/quinnipiac-university-poll/new-j=
ersey/release-detail?ReleaseID=3D2068>
 and Florida
<http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2014/07/24/clinton-polling-well-in-ke=
y-presidential-battleground/>,
Hillary Clinton had commanding leads over potential 2016 GOP contenders.



o   According to a Quinnipiac press release
<http://www.quinnipiac.edu/news-and-events/quinnipiac-university-poll/ohio/=
release-detail?ReleaseID=3D2066>
on
the Ohio poll, "Secretary Clinton, on the other hand, is almost everyone's
favorite candidate.=E2=80=9D



